menu "Screen board"

choice SCREEN_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    default SCREEN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_7
    help
        Selects the display driver. Everything else is board independent.

        Three boards have been and gone -- an Adafruit Feather ESP32-S3 TFT,
        a Waveshare ESP32-S3-LCD-1.47B and a NiceMCU-32S-DEV 2.8IPS, all three
        driving an ST7789 over SPI. Their support came out with them, and
        display_st7789.c went too, having no board left to drive. It is in the
        history if another SPI panel ever turns up.

config SCREEN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_7
    bool "Elecrow CrowPanel 7.0 HMI (800x480, 16-bit RGB)"

config SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    bool "LilyGO T-Display-S3 (ST7789 320x170, 8-bit i80)"

config SCREEN_BOARD_TOUCH_LCD_35B
    bool "Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-3.5B (AXS15231B 320x480, QSPI)"

endchoice

config SCREEN_DEVICE_NAME
    string "Advertised BLE name"
    default "big" if SCREEN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_7
    default "lilly" if SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    default "visio" if SCREEN_BOARD_TOUCH_LCD_35B
    default "small"
    help
        The name this board advertises. Give each board on your desk a
        distinct name so `tell --device NAME` can pick between them.

config SCREEN_I2C_SDA
    int "I2C SDA pin"
    default 19 if SCREEN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_7
    default 18 if SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    default 8 if SCREEN_BOARD_TOUCH_LCD_35B
    default 42
    help
        The CrowPanel wires its GT911 touch controller to GPIO19/20. The
        Feather's STEMMA QT port is GPIO42/41, from Adafruit's board variant.
        The T-Display-S3 brings its bus out on GPIO18/17. The Waveshare
        1.47B wires its onboard QMI8658 to GPIO48/47. The NiceMCU brings its
        CST816D out on GPIO33/32, and the gas sensor shares that bus.

        visio (Touch-LCD-3.5B) hangs everything off one bus on GPIO8/7: the
        AXP2101 PMIC, the AXS15231B touch, a QMI8658, a PCF85063 RTC and a
        TCA9554 expander. The display itself is QSPI, not on here.

config SCREEN_I2C_SCL
    int "I2C SCL pin"
    default 20 if SCREEN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_7
    default 17 if SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    default 7 if SCREEN_BOARD_TOUCH_LCD_35B
    default 41

config SCREEN_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Panel brightness, 0 to 100"
    range 0 100
    default 100
    help
        A lit panel is the largest steady draw on these boards and most of
        what makes them warm. Full brightness is rarely needed on a desk.

config SCREEN_BUTTON_A
    int "First button pin, or -1 for none"
    default 0 if SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    default 0 if SCREEN_BOARD_TOUCH_LCD_35B
    default -1
    help
        GPIO0 is the BOOT button on both of these boards. It is a strapping
        pin, so holding it through a reset still enters the bootloader, but
        once running it is an ordinary input with a pull-up.

        With two buttons the first goes back and the second forward. With only
        one, a tap goes forward and holding it goes back, because a board with
        one button still needs both directions.

config SCREEN_BUTTON_B
    int "Second button pin, or -1 for none"
    default 14 if SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    default -1
    help
        The Waveshare 1.47B has two buttons on it but only one that software
        can see: its other key pulls EN to ground and is a hardware reset.

config SCREEN_ENV_ONLY
    bool "Show only the clock and the room readings"
    default n
    help
        Makes the board an environment logger and nothing else: the clock,
        and a page each for temperature, humidity and pressure, charted from
        the log in flash.

        Everything else is dropped from the build -- the sand, the spirit
        level, the shake timer, the stopwatch, the clock chip's own page and
        the die-against-crystal chart -- along with the IMU driver they
        needed. A board doing one job well is easier to read at a glance than
        one offering nine things, and the freed RAM goes to the charts.

        Needs a BME280 for the readings and an envlog partition to keep them.

config SCREEN_ENV_DEMO
    bool "Fill an empty log with invented readings"
    default n
    help
        Writes a few days of plausible indoor weather into an empty log, so
        the charts can be looked at before the sensors are wired.

        Only ever into an EMPTY log, and only when no sensor answered: it will
        not overwrite readings and it will not run beside real ones. Every
        record it writes carries ENV_SYNTHETIC, so invented data cannot be
        mistaken for measured data later, by a person or by a tool.

        Turn it off and erase the log once the sensors are in.

config SCREEN_VIVID_PALETTE
    bool "Use the vivid accent colours rather than the colour-blind-safe set"
    default y
    help
        The vivid set, chosen on the hardware: Okabe-Ito is a print palette
        and print is not backlit, so on a lit panel its colours read as washed
        out rather than as restrained.

        Turning this off returns the Okabe-Ito accents, which stay apart under
        the common forms of colour blindness. What is given up by leaving it on
        is that at full saturation the orange and the vermillion sit closer
        together for a deuteranope, and the charts use that pair to mean
        different things -- though never colour alone: every bar on every chart
        here carries a text label as well.

config SCREEN_CLOCK_MS
    bool "Show thousandths on the clock"
    default n if SCREEN_ENV_ONLY
    default y
    help
        Thousandths cost a redraw every frame instead of every second, which
        is most of what a board does while the clock is showing. On a display
        of Claude's usage that is a fair price for a clock that visibly runs.

        An environment logger is a quiet instrument that sits still, and a
        digit flickering thirty times a second is at odds with that -- as well
        as being the largest thing it does with its processor for no reading
        anyone takes.

config SCREEN_ROTATE_SECS
    int "Seconds on each page before moving to the next, or 0 to stay put"
    default 20 if SCREEN_BOARD_TDISPLAY_S3
    default 0
    help
        A board with no touch panel and two buttons is better at showing you
        things in turn than at being navigated. Rotation makes the pages a
        slideshow you watch rather than a menu you drive, and it replaces the
        screensaver: nothing sits still long enough to burn in, so the saver
        has nothing left to protect.

        A button press holds the current page for a minute before the
        rotation picks up again. Pages struck off the round on the menu, and
        the ones that are never in it -- the sand, the level, a running
        stopwatch -- are left out here too.

        0 leaves the display wherever it was last put, which is what the big
        panel and the Feather do.

        visio stays put on Pip, her home face, rather than rotating: a desk
        familiar you glance at is not a slideshow. Her other subsystems become
        pages you can reach once touch is wired.

config SCREEN_I2C_AUX_SDA
    int "Second I2C SDA pin, or -1 for none"
    default -1
    help
        A second bus, for chips added to pins the first one cannot reach.
        wave's QMI8658 is soldered to GPIO48/47 and neither comes out to her
        header, so anything wired on later -- a DS3231, say -- cannot join
        that bus. GPIO10 and GPIO11 are on her header and used by nothing.

        -1 means the board has no second bus, which is not a failure: the
        drivers simply do without.

config SCREEN_I2C_AUX_SCL
    int "Second I2C SCL pin, or -1 for none"
    default -1

endmenu
